WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.167
WILFRED MCCLAY: It's different
than the kind of acts

00:00:02.167 --> 00:00:04.500
of imagination that go with
fiction, because history

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:05.566
actually happened.

00:00:05.566 --> 00:00:08.542
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:14.615
We rode in on a horse
that we didn't create.

00:00:14.615 --> 00:00:15.990
Well, you ought
to know something

00:00:15.990 --> 00:00:18.750
about that horse and the
horses before it and before it

00:00:18.750 --> 00:00:19.732
and before it.

00:00:19.732 --> 00:00:20.174
SPEAKER 1: Well, come on.

00:00:20.174 --> 00:00:21.174
What are we waiting for?

00:00:21.174 --> 00:00:24.270
WILFRED MCCLAY: If we look
at the times we're living in,

00:00:24.270 --> 00:00:28.080
the graph goes,
vroom, like this,

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:32.619
in terms of every measure of
material prosperity longevity,

00:00:32.619 --> 00:00:34.200
health, et cetera.

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:36.150
But we don't know
that if we don't know

00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:38.460
what things were like before.

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:40.510
[CHATTER]

00:00:40.510 --> 00:00:43.870
SPEAKER 2: It's up to
us to enjoy life in full

00:00:43.870 --> 00:00:45.165
and to make it count.

00:00:45.165 --> 00:00:46.582
WILFRED MCCLAY:
There's a tendency

00:00:46.582 --> 00:00:50.700
to think we can decide questions
of principle by reference only

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:52.390
to abstractions.

00:00:52.390 --> 00:00:54.900
Now, history is concrete.

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:57.930
History fills in the blanks
with real world events

00:00:57.930 --> 00:01:00.940
and real people, things
that actually happened.

00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:04.090
And we can test
out our principles

00:01:04.090 --> 00:01:06.415
by seeing how they
operate in history.

00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:14.630
LARRY P. ARNN: Good history
would be an accurate picture

00:01:14.630 --> 00:01:16.770
of what happened in the past.

00:01:16.770 --> 00:01:19.020
There's a sympathy
for the people

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:21.880
so that one can learn from them.

00:01:21.880 --> 00:01:24.540
It gives us a
fixity that is made

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:27.750
of things that, at the
time, were constantly

00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:29.244
shifting and hard to estimate.

00:01:29.244 --> 00:01:31.286
FRANKLIN D. ROOSEVELT: It
is possible, of course.

00:01:31.286 --> 00:01:33.655
JOHN F. KENNEDY: --of
landing a man on the moon

00:01:33.655 --> 00:01:35.238
and returning him
safely to the earth.

00:01:37.353 --> 00:01:38.770
WILFRED MCCLAY: I
think US history

00:01:38.770 --> 00:01:44.940
is a really extraordinary moment
in the history of the world.

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:48.010
The story of this country is
one of the most interesting,

00:01:48.010 --> 00:01:51.210
exciting, compelling
stories that you

00:01:51.210 --> 00:01:54.480
could tell about human
life on this planet.

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:57.390
Returning to our
history and seeing

00:01:57.390 --> 00:01:59.670
why things happened
the way they did

00:01:59.670 --> 00:02:04.290
can give us some insight that
we might not otherwise have.

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:06.740
The study of
history expands you.

00:02:06.740 --> 00:02:11.750
You learn to empathize
with very different ways

00:02:11.750 --> 00:02:14.640
of living in the past.

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:16.970
I think that's fun.

00:02:16.970 --> 00:02:20.100
I'd put it up against anything.

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:26.870
I'm Wilfred McClay, professor
of history and letters.

00:02:26.870 --> 00:02:29.475
This is our course
in American history.

00:02:29.475 --> 00:02:30.850
RONALD REAGAN:
And determination.

00:02:30.850 --> 00:02:32.017
WILFRED MCCLAY: Let's begin.

00:02:32.017 --> 00:02:36.300
[MUSIC PLAYING]

